umbency": 0.0, "money": 1.55}, "rating_margin": 12.0, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.92, "baseline_margin": 9.66, "analyst_adjustment": -1.16, "key_factors": ["Klobuchar's roughly 20-to-1 fundraising edge and statewide name recognition", "Consistent polling lead plus Likely/Solid D consensus from raters and 93-95% market pricing", "Open seat after Walz's retirement removes the incumbency advantage", "Public safety now the top issue after Twin Cities violence, giving Demuth an opening"], "rationale": "Klobuchar has led every published poll, by margins of two to fifteen points, and holds a roughly twenty-to-one money advantage, while all seven race ratings and all three prediction markets place the open seat firmly on the Democratic side. Tightening since August around public safety keeps this likely rather than safe.", "watch": "If the October 21 debate or a sustained public-safety focus consolidates undecided voters behind Demuth, the margin could narrow meaningfully in the final weeks.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Tim Walz (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's governorship is open with Tim Walz retiring, and Senator Amy Klobuchar enters as the clear favorite over state Representative Lisa Demuth. Polling since June has consistently shown Klobuchar ahead, with margins ranging from two points in a Republican-sponsored September survey to fifteen points in August. Aggregated estimates put her lead near nine to eleven points. Seven race ratings all place the contest on the Democratic side, from likely to solid Democratic, and prediction markets price her win probability in the mid-nineties.\n\nKlobuchar has raised roughly seven million dollars to Demuth's four hundred thousand, giving her a dominant position in the most expensive governor's race in state history. The campaign's main fault line is public safety, which has risen after high-profile Twin Cities violence, while Demuth presses economic and business-climate themes. A Green Party candidate draws minimal support. An October 21 debate remains the last major scheduled event.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9683, "margin": 10.03}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9647, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.931, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9507, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9476, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9456, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9491, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9656, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9546, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.9536, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.9443, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.9456, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.9461, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.9453, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.9445, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.9397, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.9598, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.927, "margin": 8.5}]}